# Local development

This is a pnpm + Lerna TypeScript monorepo. Packages publish from `dist/` (`main` points at `dist/index.js`). Examples and other packages consume **built output**, not `src/`.

## Run the viewer locally

- Use `examples/simple`: `npm run build-dev` (webpack-dev-server on `http://localhost:8080/`).
- Pass a ShapeDiver model with query params:

```
http://localhost:8080/?ticket=<ticket>&modelViewUrl=<gateway-url>
```

- The example page exposes `window.SDV` after `bundle.js` loads.

## Windows scripts

Package scripts call `bash`. On this machine `bash` on `PATH` is `C:\Windows\System32\bash.exe`, the WSL launcher, and it fails when no distribution is installed. Run those scripts with Git bash as the script shell for that command only: `$env:npm_config_script_shell = "C:\Program Files\Git\bin\bash.exe"`. Do not change the saved npm config.

## Rebuild after source changes

- After editing a workspace package, run `npm run build` **in that package** (for example `rendering-engine/rendering-engine-threejs`). The simple example’s webpack watches `dist/` and will rebundle.
- Do **not** run root `npm run build` for a one-package change; it builds ~29 packages and is slow.
- Root `npm run build` is for a full monorepo compile before publish or a broad check.

## Tests

- Root `npm test` runs Playwright against the **hosted** viewer (`https://viewer.shapediver.com/v3/latest/` by default), not local `src/` or `dist/`.
- Do not use that suite to verify uncommitted local code. Drive `localhost:8080` (or `test-cdn`) instead.
- Staging CDN: `VIEWER_TEST_ENV=staging`. Tests load `test-cdn/index.html`, which exposes `SDV` without auto-creating a session.

## Performance tests

- `pnpm test:performance` runs `tests/performance/*.perf.ts` against a locally built `examples/test-cdn`. Build that example first (`npm run build` in `examples/test-cdn`). The script is not part of `pnpm test` and does not run on CI.
- These tests assert counts and identities: UUID creations, draw calls, canvas pixels, material side and three.js type, shader source, `preserveDrawingBuffer`, shared buffers, worker constructions. They do not assert milliseconds or FPS.
- The suite launches installed Chrome (`channel: "chrome"`) with `--use-angle=d3d11`. Playwright's bundled Chromium on this machine is SwiftShader. This viewer turns shadows off when the renderer string is SwiftShader, so the contact-shadow test would not exercise the code.
- Do not run the suite in a Playwright Docker image to make the numbers exact. Headless Chrome there is SwiftShader or an unpredictable GPU, and a CPU or memory limit does not make frame times identical. A shadow test must fail when `UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL` contains SwiftShader. That result is not a measurement.

If you learn more about how to run or test this repo, update this file or add a new rule under `.cursor/rules/`.
